        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/5081872/items/ZTE3DH2A> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:BBA26 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "A clear and common understanding of key terms such as nature, biodiversity, ecosystem services, natural capital, nature’s contributions to people, and businesses are needed as a starting point to understand the interaction of businesses and financial institutions with nature." ;
    dcterms:identifier "1.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Key concepts" .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/BBA26-KM2> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A3>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B13>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B7>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C8> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:BBA26 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "While some businesses take actions that are beneficial for biodiversity, inadequate or perverse incentives perpetuate business as usual and create barriers to businesses and others taking action that could halt and reverse biodiversity loss and addressing its interconnection with climate change and pollution. The conditions under which different businesses operate vary and do not affect all sectors or businesses equally. Currently, the existing rewards and penalties are not adequate to drive sufficient action by businesses for the conservation and sustainable use of biodiversity and the fair and equitable sharing of benefits from the utilization of genetic resources and associated traditional knowledge. Businesses often fail to internalize the negative impacts of their actions on biodiversity. Many policies either encourage business activities that are harmful to biodiversity or prevent behaviour that is beneficial to it. For example, large subsidies are directed towards business activities that drive biodiversity loss, often with the support of lobbying by businesses and trade associations with vested interests. In 2023, global public and private financial flows with direct negative impacts on nature were estimated at $7.3 trillion, including environmentally harmful public subsidies and private investment in high-impact sectors. Of that total, private finance accounted for around two thirds ($4.9 trillion), while public spending on environmentally harmful subsidies made up the remaining $2.4 trillion. By contrast, only around $220 billion in public and private financial flows in 2023 were directed towards activities that contribute to the conservation and sustainable use of biodiversity. These figures highlight both the opportunity and the need to align financial flows to support biodiversity outcomes. Where compliance by businesses and enforcement efforts are lacking, the effective implementation of laws and regulations is undermined. Furthermore, because business disclosures are often voluntary and not widespread, businesses are usually not held accountable. Businesses often lack the data and knowledge to quantify their impacts and dependencies on biodiversity, and much of the relevant scientific literature is not written for a business audience. Lack of transparency across value chains, including with regard to the risks and opportunities related to the sustainability of resource extraction, use and reuse and waste management, is a further barrier to action. In addition, ecological processes such as ecosystem regeneration do not align with the time pressures on business decision-making and timescales for investment returns and reporting, which place heavy emphasis on quarterly earnings and annual reporting." ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "The current external conditions in which businesses operate are not always compatible with achieving a just and sustainable future and perpetuate systemic risks {A1, A2, A3, A5, B7, B13, C8}." .

<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/BBA26-KM5> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A5>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C3> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:BBA26 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """The science base for understanding and measuring business impacts and dependencies is already being used to guide business actions. Some businesses already apply existing methods, knowledge and data; as the science base improves, all businesses will be able to take action. The understanding of how to apply methods for impact assessment is more advanced than for assessments of dependencies. The availability of measurement approaches and underlying data varies by business sector, ecological realm and jurisdiction. The application and uptake of methods is low and uneven across and within business sectors and varies across regions and countries, reflecting differences in data availability and technical capacity.

Different knowledge communities offer credible methodologies that can be applied across diverse business contexts, each contributing distinct approaches to addressing impacts and dependencies. Many aspects of biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people may be relevant to measuring business impacts and dependencies, including aspects at the genetic, species and ecosystem levels and the diverse benefits associated with nature’s contributions to people. Which aspects should be measured depends on the context, location and action to be taken or the decision to be informed. As a result, a combination of methods or metrics, which may include scientific, Indigenous and local knowledge, will often be necessary to fully capture the impacts and dependencies of business on biodiversity and nature’s contributions to people.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM5" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Methods, knowledge and data for measuring impacts and dependencies already exist and can further inform decisions and actions, directly and in the value chain {A5, B8, C1, C3}." .

