<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/key/BBA26-KM6> a ipbes:KeyMessage ;
    ipbes:BackgroundMessage <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-A8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-B8>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C1>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C2>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C4>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/bgm/BBA26-C7> ;
    ipbes:Illustration <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/il/BBA26-TableSPM.3> ;
    ipbes:Report ipbes:BBA26 ;
    ipbes:hasDescription """No single method is suitable for all business decisions (Table SPM.3). Different methods and metrics are needed for distinct sectors, decision-making levels and purposes of measurement.

Decisions at the operations level require site-specific information and data, which may be gathered using bottom-up approaches, including location-based observations, participatory mapping and monitoring, and spatial analysis built on the data from those sources. Such approaches can make use of data and metrics representing local values and the knowledge, rights and interests of other groups, including Indigenous Peoples and local communities. Bottom-up approaches can provide greater accuracy at local scales and can show how outcomes change in response to concrete actions or activities on the ground. Cumulative impacts, which arise from the aggregated effects of businesses and other actors at a location over time, are better assessed for an area such as a site, landscape or seascape than for a particular business.

Top-down methods include life-cycle approaches and macro-scale environmental economic models appropriate for decisions at the portfolio, corporate and value chain levels. Depending on the purpose of measurement, they can be conducted with lower-resolution spatial data but wider geographical scale. Portfolio-level decisions can be guided by globally comparable methods where there is sufficient resolution within sectors (e.g., differentiating according to geography or product type). Corporate-level decisions can be informed by methods that use company-specific information. Value chain-level decisions can make use of life-cycle approaches that assess potential impacts of value chains, although they are less relevant for assessing dependencies. Top-down methods provide an assessment of potential impacts and dependencies and inform decisions through screening and comparing options. However, those methods may have limited ability to track progress resulting from specific business actions. Top-down approaches using coarse-resolution data often fail to reflect local ecological values and are less able to represent the diverse values of nature, including the values and interests of Indigenous Peoples and local communities.

As a result, important areas for the future development of top-down approaches include higher‑resolution spatial and temporal data and better interweaving with diverse values of nature for understanding dependencies, impacts, risks and opportunities.""" ;
    dcterms:identifier "KM6" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Different methods to measure and manage impacts and dependencies are needed for different sectors, decision-making levels and business purposes {A8, B8, C1, C2, C4, C7}." .

